**1. A self-retracting lifeline apparatus comprising:**  

a housing configured for attachment to an anchorage;  

a reel rotatably disposed within the housing;  

a lifeline wound about the reel and having a proximal portion coupled to the reel and a distal portion extendable from and retractable into the housing;  

a retraction mechanism biasing the reel toward retraction of the lifeline;  

a speed-responsive braking mechanism configured to arrest rotation of the reel upon extension of the lifeline at or above a predetermined speed; and  

an abrasion-resistant tie-back section disposed at the distal portion of the lifeline, the tie-back section including a flexible load-bearing member and an abrasion-resistant protective covering extending along at least a portion of the flexible load-bearing member, the tie-back section being configured to wrap around a structural anchorage and to be releasably coupled back to the lifeline apparatus to form a closed anchorage loop,  

wherein the tie-back section has a minimum tensile breaking strength of at least 8,000 pounds when tested in accordance with applicable static-strength testing provisions of ANSI/ASSE Z359.13-2009.

Below is an illustrative U.S.-style independent apparatus claim. It expresses the tie-back capability through structural and functional limitations rather than relying solely on the phrase “compliant with ANSI/ASSE Z359.13-2009.”

> **1. A self-retracting lifeline assembly comprising:**  
> a housing;  
>   
> a drum rotatably supported by the housing;  
>   
> a lifeline having a first end coupled to the drum and a second end outside the housing, the lifeline being windable onto and extendable from the drum;  
>   
> a retraction mechanism configured to bias the drum in a direction that retracts the lifeline into the housing;  
>   
> a braking mechanism configured to resist rotation of the drum in response to extension of the lifeline at or above a predetermined rate;  
>   
> a connector coupled to the second end of the lifeline; and  
>   
> a tie-back anchoring section extending along a distal portion of the lifeline adjacent the second end, the tie-back anchoring section comprising a load-bearing tensile member and an abrasion-resistant protective member surrounding at least part of the load-bearing tensile member,  
>   
> wherein the tie-back anchoring section is sufficiently flexible to be routed around an anchorage, and the connector is releasably engageable with an engagement portion of the assembly to form a closed anchoring loop around the anchorage, the closed anchoring loop being configured to transfer a fall-arrest load through the load-bearing tensile member while the protective member contacts the anchorage, and  
>   
> wherein the tie-back anchoring section and the connector, when arranged as the closed anchoring loop, are configured to satisfy the strength, dynamic-performance, and tie-back-use requirements applicable to a tie-back lanyard under ANSI/ASSE Z359.13-2009.

A version avoiding direct incorporation of the standard into the claim could end instead with:

> wherein the tie-back anchoring section, the connector, and the engagement portion are collectively configured to withstand a predetermined static load and to arrest a test mass under predetermined dynamic test conditions without separation of the closed anchoring loop.

The applicable numerical loads, test mass, free-fall distance, maximum arresting force, and conditioning protocols could then be recited in dependent claims based on the exact provisions of the standard and the product configuration.

Useful dependent-claim subjects include:

- the protective member comprising a replaceable abrasion sleeve;
- the tie-back section having a visually distinguishable boundary or wear indicator;
- the engagement portion comprising a D-ring, eye, loop, or reinforced connector-receiving region;
- a connector gate that is self-closing and self-locking;
- an energy absorber positioned between the drum and the tie-back section;
- the tensile member being wire rope, synthetic rope, or webbing;
- dimensions limiting the portion permitted to contact the anchorage; and
- specific static and dynamic performance values taken from the correct edition of ANSI/ASSE Z359.13.

Because ANSI/ASSE Z359.13 principally addresses personal energy absorbers and lanyards, while self-retracting devices are addressed more directly by other Z359 standards, the specification should explain precisely which Z359.13 tie-back requirements apply and how they interact with the requirements governing the self-retracting unit. A patent claim should not depend only on “compliant with” language if the distinguishing structure can be stated expressly.
